#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b54a1906-4418-558c-9b9c-70f6e198cd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343580} {:answer/id #uuid "0b44b287-f452-5531-9f51-50420157ee60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343581} {:answer/id #uuid "9845e8b3-3a41-5521-88bc-2a40aafd1faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343582} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dbe8dd-0ad3-5585-a145-aa06f0e310bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343583} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd60022-6510-51e0-ae95-18db32723735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b54a1906-4418-558c-9b9c-70f6e198cd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343580} :answer-field/id #uuid "285c6789-0ea2-580c-8c00-303d243386d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f8d78f-9334-58cc-8d51-450e53ee811c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140385" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df618613-22e4-564d-ae8a-9c0689e03728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343587} {:answer/id #uuid "38f4d14c-c156-5172-b0e1-a6e99297927d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343588} {:answer/id #uuid "8517f369-145a-563d-a9c0-43f709f38d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343589} {:answer/id #uuid "d5056bcd-c91a-5e4c-8991-8d8e8bd00de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343590} {:answer/id #uuid "809087b6-e970-5be0-85f2-145574d9ec50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809087b6-e970-5be0-85f2-145574d9ec50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343591} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bbfec4-3eb6-5ea8-89d6-d25e88cedc12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ae540d5-f0cb-5097-abf0-3d9578dec947" :question/math-academy-id "q-140388" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b6958d-be11-5741-a6f0-77ea692917c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343594} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9e93fd-495b-5086-a59c-27cb55a78afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343595} {:answer/id #uuid "61303b9a-f9b8-560f-bf1e-4d58fb8e519c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343596} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1e0b67-2155-5b03-966d-7a1bc4c2e07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343597} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeee794-96ef-55f1-b37a-58a76cbd0496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61303b9a-f9b8-560f-bf1e-4d58fb8e519c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343596} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5021ae-9477-5d04-ad70-c7dbe0bbf486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dca6f83-e205-581e-970a-5088200d5c7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39189" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(12-\\lambda )-(-5)(10) & =0 \\\\ -24+2\\lambda -12\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+50 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +26 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(26)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5-\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7+\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -5 & 7+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-7+\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5+\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(7+\\text{i})(7-\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fec7827-bad7-547f-8389-4e4d0e10f480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343601} {:answer/id #uuid "07c591b3-2963-54f9-b371-6bcff25ea2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343602} {:answer/id #uuid "97bc9eb9-99fb-5f8a-bec6-4f6938aedf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343603} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce75948-bf18-5d34-9b4b-899ae78011ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343604} {:answer/id #uuid "29721852-3731-5992-bbbd-6aff9aebc555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29721852-3731-5992-bbbd-6aff9aebc555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605} :answer-field/id #uuid "a028c170-ff03-5d7a-a774-14680687814a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44d22880-1719-5f05-b9a2-8b81c0330bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81030" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0192428f-d96f-526b-a0c5-e2b086a252b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343608} {:answer/id #uuid "8e09cb6b-4880-5e9e-93c9-49781e63881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343609} {:answer/id #uuid "d271ba02-9a28-51b9-a7ae-7eaddc5376a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343610} {:answer/id #uuid "87a3be2a-00dc-523a-a60e-e07812fe916a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343611} {:answer/id #uuid "3411e5dd-6676-512f-b57b-f7685ed2caf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e09cb6b-4880-5e9e-93c9-49781e63881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343609} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f6cc44-b56b-56a9-84f3-a6bde3a09977" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c27b7b8-e30b-536b-a69b-9501e33a6b11" :question/math-academy-id "q-81184" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$"}]}
